From b50da964abf4922178fc29c490311f8fd7014683 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonni Madekivi <wnt@reaktor.fi>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 17:25:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] macfb: absolute pointer over classic Mac OS low-memory
 globals (ramabs/1)

The kernel-hive fleet reaches a 1:1 absolute pointer on emulated machines by
CLOSING A LOOP: read the guest's pointer position out of a display adapter's
hardware-cursor registers, inject relative counts, converge. The Quadra 800 has
no hardware cursor -- classic Mac OS composites the cursor into the framebuffer
in software -- so that recipe has no sensor on this machine.

It does not need one. Mac OS keeps its pointer state in low-memory globals at
fixed, documented addresses, identity-mapped to guest-physical RAM on q800, so
the device model can read AND write the guest's own idea of where the pointer
is. This engine therefore does not converge on a target: it STATES the target
using the OS's own documented cursor-warp idiom, then VERIFIES by reading back
what the OS did with it.

The consequence worth recording is that the HOTSPOT LEAVES THE CONTROL PATH.
`Mouse` ($0830) holds the POINTER; QuickDraw subtracts the current cursor's
hotspot only when it blits the sprite. A loop whose sensor is a sprite origin
must measure the hotspot or it builds a magnet that drags the pointer at a
stationary target. Here there is nothing to measure -- we write pointer
coordinates and read pointer coordinates -- and the hotspot is read from
$0884 for telemetry only, so an external framebuffer check knows where to
expect the sprite.

The one trap, measured rather than assumed: DO NOT ALSO WRITE `Mouse`. The
cursor VBL task computes `Mouse := (RawMouse & MouseMask) + MouseOffset` and
repaints only when that differs from the `Mouse` it already had. Pre-writing
`Mouse` makes its own change detector say "nothing moved" -- it clears CrsrNew
and draws nothing, while every global reads back exactly correct and the
pointer sits still. `Mouse` is the task's OUTPUT and this engine's sensor.

Two rules are inherited from the hardware-cursor stations. A quiescent sensor
is not a converged sensor: a RAM read always succeeds and always looks
plausible, with no "cursor enabled" bit to even partially cover you, so a MOVEA
is confirmed only when `Mouse` READS BACK EQUAL to what was written. And values
are sanity-bounded where they are READ, not where they are used: `CrsrPin` must
be a sane rect that agrees with the framebuffer geometry before any position is
trusted, a position must lie inside it, a hotspot must lie inside 16x16. A
failed bound refuses the write rather than guessing, and STAT answers "valid=0"
rather than quoting a number the engine never observed.

The verify timer is QEMU_CLOCK_VIRTUAL, which does not tick while the guest is
stopped -- and the station that uses this starts `-loadvm golden -S` under TCG.
A read-back wait against a stopped runstate would never complete and would loop
the daemon forever on its own resync preamble, so a MOVEA issued while paused
writes guest RAM (durable; the VBL task consumes it on resume), acks, and drops
the verification, counting it as paused= with valid=0.

No vmstate field is added, so an existing checkpoint still restores against a
binary carrying this patch. Armed only by `-global nubus-macfb.ptrctl=<chardev>`
(the property is on both the nubus and sysbus variants; `-M q800` instantiates
the nubus one). Buttons ride the emulated ADB mouse as button-only edges so the
driver keeps ownership of MBState/MBTicks and double-click timing; only the
POSITION bypasses ADB.

Wire protocol `ramabs/1`, shared with the rhapsody station's guest-RAM engine:
HELLO, <seq> MOVEA x y, DOWN<n>/UP<n>, SYNC, STAT; everything guest-specific
lives here rather than in the protocol or the daemon.
